    ctaHref={PDF_PATH}
    secondaryCta="Or call: (210) 981-4098"
    secondaryHref="tel:2109814098"
    imageSrc={HERO}
    imageAlt="Dedicated home theater with leather recliners, a projection screen and on-wall speakers, built by KZ Audio & Video in Canyon Lake"
    imagePosition="object-center"
    compact={true}
  />

  <TrustBar />

  <!-- Self-contained answer, written to be quoted -->
  <section class="section bg-slate-surface">
    <div class="container-main">
      <p class="overline mb-3">The Short Answer</p>
      <h2 class="font-display text-h2 text-warm-white mb-6">How Do You Plan a Home Theater?</h2>
      <p class="text-body-lg text-stone max-w-3xl leading-relaxed mb-6">
        Plan a home theater in this order: the room, the seats, the screen, the sound, the wiring, and the gear last. Decide first whether it is a dedicated room you can darken or a media room that shares space with the house. Then set the seating, because the distance from the seats to the screen wall sets the screen size: for a 4K picture, sit about 1 to 1.5 times the screen's diagonal away. Pick a projector if you can control the light and a large TV if you cannot. Choose the surround format (5.1, 7.1 or Dolby Atmos) and the speaker types: in-wall and in-ceiling for a clean dedicated room, on-wall, bookshelf or tower where the walls stay closed. In a new build, the low voltage wiring goes in before drywall, and the speakers, projector and screen go in after. Finish with dimmable lighting, simple control, and calibration of the picture and the sound.
      </p>
      <p class="text-body text-stone max-w-3xl leading-relaxed">
        <strong class="text-warm-white">The order matters more than the budget.</strong> Get it right and a modest system feels like a real theater. Get it wrong and the best gear still feels like a TV in a spare room.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Steps 1 to 5 -->
  <section class="section bg-charcoal">
    <div class="container-main">
      <p class="overline mb-3">The Plan</p>
      <h2 class="font-display text-h2 text-warm-white mb-12">Steps 1 to 5: Room, Seats, Screen and Sound</h2>
      <ol class="grid grid-cols-1 md:grid-cols-2 gap-6">
        {firstSteps.map(({ t, lead, points }, i) => (
          <li class="card p-7 flex flex-col gap-4">
            <span class="font-display font-bold text-gold" style="font-size:1.75rem;line-height:1;" aria-hidden="true">{String(i + 1).padStart(2, '0')}</span>
            <h3 class="font-sans font-semibold text-h4 text-warm-white">Step {i + 1}: {t}</h3>
            <p class="text-body text-stone leading-relaxed">{lead}</p>
            <ul class="flex flex-col gap-3">
              {points.map(([b, d]) => (
                <li class="flex gap-3 text-body-sm text-stone leading-relaxed">
                  <Fragment set:html={check} />
                  <span><strong class="text-warm-white">{b}</strong> {d}</span>
                </li>
              ))}
            </ul>
          </li>
        ))}
      </ol>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- Step 6: speaker types -->
  <section class="section bg-slate-surface">
    <div class="container-main">
      <p class="overline mb-3">Step 6: Speaker Types</p>
      <h2 class="font-display text-h2 text-warm-white mb-6" style="max-width:24ch;">In-Wall, On-Wall, Bookshelf, Tower or In-Ceiling: Which Speakers Should You Use?</h2>
      <p class="text-body-lg text-stone max-w-3xl leading-relaxed mb-12">
        The sound format tells you how many speakers. The speaker type tells you how they live in the room. There are five, and most theaters mix two or three.
      </p>
      <div class="grid grid-cols-1 lg:grid-cols-12 gap-10 items-start">
        <figure class="lg:col-span-5">
          <div class="aspect-[4/5] overflow-hidden rounded-lg">
            <img
              src={SPEAKER_PHOTO}
              alt="In-wall speaker mounted flush in a Canyon Lake home theater wall below a sconce, before the grille goes on"
              class="w-full h-full object-cover object-center"
              loading="lazy"
              width="1170"
              height="2532"
            />
          </div>
          <figcaption class="text-body-sm text-stone mt-3">An in-wall speaker in a Canyon Lake theater, before the painted grille goes on.</figcaption>
        </figure>
        <div class="lg:col-span-7 flex flex-col gap-5">
          {speakerTypes.map(({ name, what, where, best }) => (
            <article class="card p-6 flex flex-col gap-2">
              <h3 class="font-sans font-semibold text-h4 text-warm-white">{name}</h3>
              <p class="text-body-sm text-stone leading-relaxed">{what}</p>
              <p class="text-body-sm text-stone leading-relaxed"><strong class="text-warm-white">Where it goes:</strong> {where}</p>
              <p class="text-body-sm text-stone leading-relaxed"><strong class="text-warm-white">Best for:</strong> {best}</p>
            </article>
          ))}
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="card p-7 mt-10 max-w-4xl">
        <h3 class="font-sans font-semibold text-h4 text-warm-white mb-3">How to choose</h3>
        <p class="text-body text-stone leading-relaxed">
          A dedicated theater: in-wall and in-ceiling, so nothing clutters the room. A media room or living room: in-wall or on-wall up front, in-ceiling for Atmos. A room you cannot open up, or a system you plan to take with you: bookshelf or tower. Whatever you pick, keep the center channel from the same family as the left and right speakers, so a voice sounds the same as it moves across the screen. More on placement: <a href="/surround-sound-speaker-placement/" class="text-gold hover:text-warm-white transition-colors underline underline-offset-2 cursor-pointer">where surround speakers go</a>.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

