  <!-- Kelly's own job story, 2026-10-25. The cause was never proven: the tower is a strong pattern,
       so the copy states what we heard and tried, not a mechanism. -->
  <section class="section bg-slate-surface">
    <div class="container-main">
      <div class="grid grid-cols-1 lg:grid-cols-12 gap-12 items-start">
        <div class="lg:col-span-7">
          <p class="overline mb-3">From the Field</p>
          <h2 class="font-display text-h2 text-warm-white mb-6">When a Radio Station Came Through the Surround Sound</h2>
          <p class="text-body-lg text-stone leading-relaxed mb-5">
            In one living room we wired for surround sound, a radio station came through the speakers every time the receiver was on, even with it set to the TV input. It sounded like an AM station, and an AM radio tower stands just outside that neighborhood. In another house there, our tone tester (the tool we use to trace wires) picked up a radio broadcast every time we pressed its button. Yet that house, which we believe sits a little closer to the tower, ran surround sound with no trouble.
          </p>
          <p class="text-body text-stone leading-relaxed mb-8">
            Nothing we tried stopped it. So we changed the system instead of fighting the house: a Sonos soundbar with Sonos rear speakers, surround sound with no receiver at all. That ended it.
          </p>
          <h3 class="font-sans font-semibold text-h4 text-warm-white mb-3">A second house: one under-cabinet light</h3>
          <p class="text-body text-stone leading-relaxed mb-5">
            In another home, an older stereo with two tower speakers picked up interference on the radio whenever one under-cabinet light was on. Those lights are low voltage, and their transformer sat in the attic, where the speaker wires also ran. When the owner streamed music through the same stereo, using a streaming device we added, the noise never showed up. Only the radio did it, and only with that one light on. Clip-on chokes on the speaker wires did nothing there either. The fix was simple: leave that light off while the radio is on.
          </p>
          <p class="text-body text-stone leading-relaxed">
            <strong class="text-warm-white">The lesson:</strong> in both houses the clip-on chokes did nothing. What worked was a different system in one and a light switch in the other, not a family living with the noise.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="lg:col-span-5 flex flex-col gap-5">
          <article class="card p-7">
            <h3 class="font-sans font-semibold text-h4 text-warm-white mb-4">The first house: what we tried</h3>
            <ul class="flex flex-col gap-3">
              {["A couple of different receivers", "An electrician's check of the house's ground (it tested good)", "Running it with and without the ground", "Noise filters", "Clip-on ferrite chokes on the speaker wires (they did nothing)"].map((t) => (
                <li class="flex gap-3 text-body-sm text-stone leading-relaxed">
                  <svg class="flex-shrink-0 mt-1 text-stone" width="16" height="16" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><line x1="18" y1="6" x2="6" y2="18"></line><line x1="6" y1="6" x2="18" y2="18"></line></svg>
                  <span>{t}</span>
                </li>
              ))}
            </ul>
          </article>
          <article class="card p-7">
            <h3 class="font-sans font-semibold text-h4 text-warm-white mb-4">The first house: what worked</h3>
            <p class="flex gap-3 text-body-sm text-stone leading-relaxed">
              <Fragment set:html={check} />
              <span>A Sonos soundbar and Sonos rear speakers: surround sound with no receiver in the room.</span>
            </p>
          </article>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

